at your feet is baking. So it’s not a secret realm only known to scientists with balloons. It’s directly responsible for things you can see and feel: hailstones, ice-cold airplane windows, contrails, and towering icy cloud tops. The freezing sky is hidden from direct skin-sensation, but it’s not invisible in practice. It’s always there, doing its work, dropping ice on your head and painting streaks across the blue. It sounds bizarre—almost fictional—but your own experience has already brushed against it.
